00:11:33.530 --> 00:11:34.610
you wrote.

258
00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:35.050
Yes.

259
00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:35.850
Yes.

260
00:11:36.530 --> 00:11:43.250
Chair Model, you remember Chair Model, where Michael falls in love with a woman in a magazine

261
00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:47.730
and finds out she's died and he goes to her grave.

262
00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:49.930
It's as if he lost his wife for 50 years.

263
00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:53.550
Paul Lieberstein wrote a lot.

264
00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:54.430
I love that episode.

265
00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:57.750
Paul is the source of the darkness on that one.

266
00:11:58.170 --> 00:12:02.030
And look, I wrote a lot of very joyous stuff too.

267
00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:04.110
I wrote a lot of The Jim and Pam.

268
00:12:05.090 --> 00:12:09.690
I wrote Threat Level Midnight, which is a very joyful episode.

269
00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:14.270
So it's not like I'm the Prince of Darkness on this show.

270
00:12:15.290 --> 00:12:16.830
And also, look, there were what?

271
00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:18.010
How many hundred episodes?

272
00:12:18.010 --> 00:12:21.230
I worked on more than half of them.

273
00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:25.630
So I'm not just this dark figure.

274
00:12:25.630 --> 00:12:26.690
I'm proud of it.

275
00:12:26.730 --> 00:12:28.190
I'm less dark now, though.

276
00:12:28.190 --> 00:12:31.830
Because, again, I do hear this and I do go, oh, my God.

277
00:12:32.270 --> 00:12:34.470
Like, how was I capable of some of this?

278
00:12:34.630 --> 00:12:38.770
Like, especially when I when I see Ryan Howard, I'm like, oh, my God.

279
00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:41.110
Like, I don't see any villain like this.

280
00:12:41.530 --> 00:12:44.830
Here's the Mashable article that comes out after this episode.

281
00:12:45.450 --> 00:12:48.630
BJ Novak, Prince of Darkness of The Office.

282
00:12:49.650 --> 00:12:50.390
No, BJ.

283
00:12:50.490 --> 00:12:52.770
We love like, my God.

284
00:12:53.050 --> 00:12:54.210
No, I think the dark is important.

285
00:12:54.210 --> 00:13:04.230
And I think I think that, you know, I remember after season one, which was six episodes, and I think all of them ended pretty darkly.

286
00:13:04.810 --> 00:13:05.230
Right.

287
00:13:05.810 --> 00:13:07.530
The pilot, Hot Girl.

288
00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:14.910
I mean, we had that nice moment at the end of Diversity, but the pilot, Diversity Day, Hot Girl, Healthcare, extremely dark.

289
00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:17.890
Basketball.

290
00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:18.730
I don't remember.

291
00:13:19.170 --> 00:13:22.130
Office Olympics and basketball maybe were a little more positive endings.

292
00:13:22.130 --> 00:13:29.650
But, you know, we had sort of a big group meeting that Greg led about, OK, what are we going to do differently in season two?

293
00:13:29.730 --> 00:13:30.450
And there was a lot different.

294
00:13:30.790 --> 00:13:37.130
And one of them was three out of four endings should be positive.

295
00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.950
And then one is just for us, meaning Paul.

296
00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:44.550
He was like, we're not going to totally sell out.

297
00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:48.150
But the British had ended dark every time, more or less.

298
00:13:48.270 --> 00:13:50.350
And we had been ending dark every time, more or less.

299
00:13:50.510 --> 00:13:56.610
And so the new thing was, you know, so I guess Scott's Tots was definitely one of those four.

300
00:13:56.730 --> 00:13:57.490
One for us.

301
00:13:58.010 --> 00:13:58.410
Yeah.

302
00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:03.190
But again, I do think it's an interesting, it is a deep, interesting one.

303
00:14:03.190 --> 00:14:06.430
And it's not that it's not part of the canon.

304
00:14:06.610 --> 00:14:07.690
It is very difficult.

305
00:14:07.690 --> 00:14:08.310
I agree.

306
00:14:08.470 --> 00:14:08.810
I agree.

307
00:14:09.650 --> 00:14:11.470
I think, though, it is Michael.

308
00:14:11.690 --> 00:14:17.790
It's honestly Michael trying to do something good, believing in himself, knowing.

309
00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:20.510
Well, I don't think he knows he's going to fall short.

310
00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:23.090
Well, look how much he believes in Pam and her art.

311
00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:23.750
I know.

312
00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:25.750
He is such a believer.

313
00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:26.570
He is.

314
00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:30.390
You know, and so his belief got ahead of him.

315
00:14:30.490 --> 00:14:34.330
And this is by far, you know, exactly like the line you quoted.

316
00:14:34.450 --> 00:14:38.570
This is the biggest consequence of all, of all of his belief.

317
00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:39.550
You know?

318
00:14:39.730 --> 00:14:39.990
Yeah.

319
00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:44.710
Now, we should remind people, or I should remind people, I wish I had.

320
00:14:44.810 --> 00:14:45.150
I admire it.

321
00:14:45.190 --> 00:14:46.230
I did not write the episode.

322
00:14:46.410 --> 00:14:50.170
This was a Lee Eisenberg and Gene Subnitsky episode.

323
00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:54.430
And I hope it doesn't sound like I'm disowning it either, because I know we talked about so

324
00:14:54.430 --> 00:14:55.110
many people are uncomfortable.

325
00:14:55.110 --> 00:15:00.170
You know, these are two of, I think, the most brilliant, you know, writers, the backbone

326
00:15:00.170 --> 00:15:01.110
of the show.

327
00:15:01.430 --> 00:15:07.790
When a lot of the original staff was burnt out, these guys took the ball.

328
00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:13.890
And a lot of the sort of center, central seasons of the office that people, that were really

329
00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:19.030
humming, that was Lee and Gene, just turning out scripts and revising scripts they're not

330
00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:19.730
even credited for.

331
00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:24.190
So those guys were really an incredible backstop for that.

332
00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:29.890
But, you know, look, Gene, in particular, I think, is the best one-liner writer in office

333
00:15:29.890 --> 00:15:30.250
history.

334
00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:33.470
He's the one who wrote, I'm not superstitious, but I'm a little stitious.

335
00:15:34.230 --> 00:15:34.350
You know?

336
00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:36.530
So I would guess he wrote that line.

337
00:15:37.610 --> 00:15:43.690
But, yeah, I mean, maybe we're getting a little off track, but that is sort of this incredible

338
00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:52.350
distillation of why we love Michael so much and why he's made everyone's life so impossibly

339
00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:56.210
difficult, which is sort of the heart and the comedy of the series.

340
00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:59.150
But it's not usually that intense, you know?

341
00:15:59.910 --> 00:16:01.270
Well, what was it like, BJ?

342
00:16:01.430 --> 00:16:05.910
You talked about, you know, Lee and Gene as the writers, but you went from writing to

343
00:16:05.910 --> 00:16:07.410
directing on The Office.

344
00:16:07.550 --> 00:16:11.770
And what was that shift like to go from the writer's room to being the director of the

345
00:16:11.770 --> 00:16:12.170
episode?

346
00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:14.510
It felt very intuitive.

347
00:16:14.590 --> 00:16:17.270
I took it very seriously, but I overprepared.

348
00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:20.770
I don't think I did any better on the first one than I did on the fifth one that I did.

349
00:16:20.770 --> 00:16:27.490
But I think that it's just very interesting what is required to leap from writer to director

350
00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:35.550
and I think to an extent actor to director is so much the confidence to picture yourself

351
00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:37.370
saying action and cut.

352
00:16:37.850 --> 00:16:42.510
Did you get any advice from anyone before you started directing?

353
00:16:43.050 --> 00:16:48.530
Yes, I got the best advice and I pass it on to anyone who directs.

354
00:16:48.530 --> 00:16:52.110
So the first piece of advice I got, first of all, it's good advice in general.

355
00:16:52.270 --> 00:16:53.270
Ask everyone for advice.

356
00:16:53.430 --> 00:16:54.990
People love giving advice.

357
00:16:56.270 --> 00:16:59.130
So anytime you wish you had advice in your show, I ask them.

358
00:16:59.270 --> 00:17:00.610
People will talk your ear off.

359
00:17:00.810 --> 00:17:03.630
So I asked everybody, what's your advice for directing?

360
00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:10.390
And I should also, we should clarify, directing an ongoing TV show is incredibly different.

361
00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:16.770
The actors know the characters, the writers know the story, the directors of photography

362
00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:18.410
and cameramen know how to shoot it.

363
00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:24.350
If you just stand there and nicely and intuitively and confidently ask everyone, how would you

364
00:17:24.350 --> 00:17:24.750
shoot this?

365
00:17:24.930 --> 00:17:25.930
How would you schedule this?

366
00:17:26.510 --> 00:17:27.870
Did that feel good to you?

367
00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:28.370
You're fine.

368
00:17:28.650 --> 00:17:31.190
Because the show is set in the pilot stage.

369
00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:36.910
Whereas a movie or a limited series, which is essentially like a long movie, you have

370
00:17:36.910 --> 00:17:40.010
to establish, is there music in the show?

371
00:17:40.250 --> 00:17:43.030
Is this a very colorful show?

372
00:17:43.030 --> 00:17:46.850
Is this a little bit of a campy wink wink show?

373
00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:48.250
Is it super deadpan?

374
00:17:48.410 --> 00:17:50.050
Like you have to establish all these things.

375
00:17:50.170 --> 00:17:54.410
But on an ongoing show, it's kind of like, I look, I took it super seriously because it's

376
00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:59.110
an amazing first step, but it's not nearly as hard, you know, coming into an ongoing

377
00:17:59.110 --> 00:17:59.350
show.

378
00:18:00.150 --> 00:18:07.510
The advice I got from Randall Einhorn, our longtime director of photography and later our most prolific

379
00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:10.170
director on the show, I said, do you have advice?

380
00:18:10.290 --> 00:18:10.910
He said, yes.

381
00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:14.210
Direct the cameras like they're actors.

382
00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:16.370
I said, what do you mean?

383
00:18:16.450 --> 00:18:23.170
He said, you'd never tell an actor, shout this line and cut it off after three seconds,

384
00:18:23.170 --> 00:18:23.910
right?

385
00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:25.190
You tell them why.

386
00:18:26.030 --> 00:18:31.170
So you never tell the camera, swing to the left on this line and punch in on that.

387
00:18:31.530 --> 00:18:34.550
You'd say, this is where we catch Pam and her lie.

388
00:18:35.110 --> 00:18:39.990
So I would creep up to her and find that moment and don't let her out.

389
00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:40.930
You know what I mean?

390
00:18:41.030 --> 00:18:43.570
And it inspires so much more creativity.

391
00:18:43.570 --> 00:18:45.010
It's so much more respectful.

392
00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:47.830
And you're more likely to get what you're looking for.

393
00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:52.770
And, you know, I've extended that to direct to everyone, like they're actors.

394
00:18:53.090 --> 00:18:55.470
You don't tell the costume department, I want him in a suit.

395
00:18:55.970 --> 00:19:01.290
You say, this is a character who's so insecure that he takes himself.

396
00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:03.590
He tries to tell the world he's important.

397
00:19:03.710 --> 00:19:04.590
I want him in a suit.

398
00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:06.030
Every day he'll wear a suit.

399
00:19:06.270 --> 00:19:10.270
Now the costume designer is so excited, you know, rather than, oh, she went to Sears and

400
00:19:10.270 --> 00:19:10.630
got a suit.

401
00:19:10.710 --> 00:19:12.390
And then you say, make it look fancier.

402
00:19:12.610 --> 00:19:14.070
No one understands what you're doing.

403
00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:14.830
No one's motivated.

404
00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:17.830
Whereas actors, that's how you talk to an actor, right?

405
00:19:17.890 --> 00:19:19.250
And people know that much more.

406
00:19:19.630 --> 00:19:24.150
So direct everyone like they're actors is, I think, the best advice I got on that show.

407
00:19:24.470 --> 00:19:29.810
Other advice I got later, the other two great pieces of advice, a friend, Lee Whannell, who

408
00:19:29.810 --> 00:19:32.550
just wrote and directed Invisible Man, which I thought was great.

409
00:19:32.730 --> 00:19:34.050
And he had been a writer forever.

410
00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:40.510
And he said that he actually took a panel on directing at the Writers Guild of America.

411
00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:42.490
And he's like, BJ, I won't do his Australian accent.

412
00:19:42.490 --> 00:19:45.970
He said, he's like, it's the kind of thing you get junk mail for.

413
00:19:46.230 --> 00:19:48.450
Come to this seminar, blah, blah, blah.

414
00:19:48.650 --> 00:19:52.230
It was the best thing I've ever been to in my life.

415
00:19:52.450 --> 00:19:53.970
It was like John Wells.

416
00:19:54.150 --> 00:19:55.250
It was Catherine Bigelow.

417
00:19:55.390 --> 00:20:00.710
And they were giving practical advice and cynical advice and inspiring advice.

418
00:20:01.230 --> 00:20:03.450
And he was like, it was the best thing I've ever been to.

419
00:20:03.530 --> 00:20:04.870
And everything I tell you is going to be from that.

420
00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:10.370
This advice was, the best thing you can do as a director is watch the movie in your head

421
00:20:10.370 --> 00:20:11.750
as much as you can.

422
00:20:11.910 --> 00:20:15.730
Whenever you're in doubt, just take a walk and watch the whole movie in your head again

423
00:20:15.730 --> 00:20:16.410
and again and again.

424
00:20:16.630 --> 00:20:21.770
And then when you're in a production meeting and someone says, what does the room look like?

425
00:20:22.190 --> 00:20:23.790
You just say what you see.

426
00:20:23.950 --> 00:20:24.950
And they figure it out.

427
00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:29.850
You don't need to know what the lens is and what the lighting scheme is.

428
00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:35.690
Um, if someone says, you know, what are they wearing or how do you want to shoot this?

429
00:20:36.050 --> 00:20:41.170
You know, you can say, oh, I picture closeups or I, I picture, oh, you're like looking from

430
00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:46.990
a distance, you know, it will have the same effect as if you studied everything in school

431
00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:49.050
because the point is to know the heart.

432
00:20:49.150 --> 00:20:50.070
So watch the movie in your head.

433
00:20:50.210 --> 00:20:56.830
The other piece of advice that's so simple, this was, I, uh, have a friend whose father

434
00:20:56.830 --> 00:20:59.830
is Al Ruddy who produced the Godfather.

435
00:21:00.450 --> 00:21:04.210
And, um, I asked him if he had any advice on directing.

436
00:21:04.450 --> 00:21:10.450
He said, you only need to know two things to direct what you want and how to get it.

437
00:21:11.710 --> 00:21:13.750
And I was like, you know what?

438
00:21:13.910 --> 00:21:20.830
I think that's the best advice I've gotten for anything because those are two very hard

439
00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:24.250
things in a way, knowing what you want is even harder.

440
00:21:25.510 --> 00:21:31.650
But all of us can approach, okay, if you know what you want and how to get it, you'll accomplish

441
00:21:31.650 --> 00:21:31.910
anything.

442
00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:37.790
And we do that for all sorts of things and how to get it includes asking someone for help

443
00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:44.910
or to understand something or to explain something or to beg them for a favor, you know, or to,

444
00:21:44.910 --> 00:21:51.190
you know, grind them down because they're just not getting it and you, whatever it is or inspiring

445
00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:53.450
them or whatever you need to get it.

446
00:21:53.730 --> 00:21:56.530
So that's, you know, what you want and how to get it.

447
00:21:56.730 --> 00:21:59.710
And then I told them, I ran into him and I was like, you gave me the best advice ever.

448
00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:00.410
And I quoted it to him.

449
00:22:00.530 --> 00:22:02.610
And he's like, oh, there's a third part.

450
00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:03.510
I forgot to tell you.

451
00:22:03.790 --> 00:22:05.690
You got to know when you got it.

452
00:22:05.770 --> 00:22:06.730
I was like, I don't like that one.

453
00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:08.730
I'm editing that one out.

454
00:22:08.730 --> 00:22:09.550
You're not wrong.

455
00:22:09.630 --> 00:22:11.510
You're not wrong, but it's not as good a quote.

456
00:22:11.750 --> 00:22:12.650
And I said that to him.

457
00:22:12.710 --> 00:22:13.430
He's like, no, no.

458
00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:16.110
I don't know what, I think he just worked on a movie with some director.

459
00:22:16.110 --> 00:22:16.690
It took forever.

460
00:22:16.970 --> 00:22:21.330
I was like, Al, no, this is not, this is Godfather part three.

461
00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:22.410
This is not as good.

462
00:22:22.550 --> 00:22:24.490
So I just quote the first two parts.

463
00:22:24.690 --> 00:22:25.730
That's so true, BJ.

464
00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:32.170
I know, you know, Angela and I changed our whole lives because we wanted to get something.

465
00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:34.330
We wanted to get a quality of life.

466
00:22:34.490 --> 00:22:36.470
We wanted to be able to make it to soccer practice.

467
00:22:36.690 --> 00:22:38.190
We want to take our kids to school.

468
00:22:38.470 --> 00:22:39.690
I didn't know you play soccer.

469
00:22:40.550 --> 00:22:40.950
Seriously.

470
00:22:41.530 --> 00:22:42.890
I'm sweeper, BJ.

471
00:22:43.430 --> 00:22:44.590
Nothing's getting into the goal.

472
00:22:45.330 --> 00:22:48.570
No, we just wanted to make our families firSt.

473
00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:57.290
And yeah, just have agency over our own schedules and our time and, and pour that into our family

474
00:22:57.290 --> 00:22:58.010
as a priority.

475
00:22:58.110 --> 00:23:00.510
And we didn't know how to get it.

476
00:23:00.630 --> 00:23:01.630
That took a long time.

477
00:23:01.830 --> 00:23:03.610
It took a lot of experimenting.

478
00:23:04.070 --> 00:23:06.590
Years of kind of griping to each other.

479
00:23:06.810 --> 00:23:07.150
Yeah.

480
00:23:07.390 --> 00:23:09.090
But then eventually we did it.

481
00:23:09.090 --> 00:23:16.070
And, and here we are interviewing you on our podcast, which is the result of our wanting

482
00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:16.870
to get something.

483
00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:18.870
And we didn't know how to do it.

484
00:23:18.970 --> 00:23:20.210
No, as you were talking.

485
00:23:20.470 --> 00:23:22.230
But it's so much easier to figure out how to do it.

486
00:23:22.390 --> 00:23:25.910
Like the worst person to work with, someone that doesn't know what they want.

487
00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:26.830
Oh my God.

488
00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:27.490
What a nightmare.

489
00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:27.870
Right.

490
00:23:28.030 --> 00:23:29.050
You knew what you wanted.

491
00:23:29.230 --> 00:23:30.890
So now you're like, I have no idea how to get this.

492
00:23:31.070 --> 00:23:31.430
Yeah.

493
00:23:31.550 --> 00:23:32.790
But like you figure that out.

494
00:23:33.110 --> 00:23:33.870
But you're right.

495
00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:44.970
You're changing clothes a million times and you're doing the role different ways and everyone,

496
00:23:45.150 --> 00:23:50.610
it really runs people into the ground, but someone with a very clear vision, then you're

497
00:23:50.610 --> 00:23:53.230
on set and you're like, okay, well, how do we do it?

498
00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:54.690
How do we make this happen?

499
00:23:54.810 --> 00:23:56.850
Like, just give me something that I can hang on to.

500
00:23:57.030 --> 00:23:57.330
Right.

501
00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.650
If your vision is clear, we'll get there.

502
00:24:01.170 --> 00:24:05.890
Oh, I have one other very cynical piece of directing advice and it does not make the top

503
00:24:05.890 --> 00:24:09.150
three, but it really worked.

504
00:24:09.410 --> 00:24:14.670
And someone said to me who had worked on The Office said, here's my advice.

505
00:24:15.510 --> 00:24:16.870
Pick one shot.

506
00:24:17.130 --> 00:24:18.530
That's a special shot.

507
00:24:18.730 --> 00:24:19.950
It doesn't have to make sense.

508
00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:22.470
It just like, it stands out.

509
00:24:22.690 --> 00:24:23.650
It doesn't quite fit.

510
00:24:24.070 --> 00:24:28.250
And the network will tell you, you're a great director because they'll notice it and they'll

511
00:24:28.250 --> 00:24:28.870
feel proud.

512
00:24:29.010 --> 00:24:30.010
And I did that.

513
00:24:30.150 --> 00:24:31.930
I think it was maybe in the webisode.

514
00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:33.030
No, I think it was in this.

515
00:24:33.030 --> 00:24:36.810
It was, there's a shot where Creed is like standing by the doorway and waiting to go

516
00:24:36.810 --> 00:24:37.350
into the room.

517
00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:41.430
I don't know which episode it was in, but literally the network is that called me personally.

518
00:24:41.610 --> 00:24:43.970
It's like, BJ, you are a great director.

519
00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:45.270
And it was that shot.

520
00:24:45.530 --> 00:24:50.950
And it was like, it was a stretch that the, I mean, it didn't break The Office style, but

521
00:24:50.950 --> 00:24:52.310
that is a cynical thing.

522
00:24:52.430 --> 00:24:58.310
And definitely when you see, like I've joked, the easiest way to win an Oscar is to be a

523
00:24:58.310 --> 00:25:01.410
cinematographer and decide to shoot the movie in glorious black and white.

524
00:25:01.410 --> 00:25:05.930
Like anything that calls attention to yourself as a director is so rewarded.

525
00:25:06.710 --> 00:25:08.630
So that is my cynical advice.

526
00:25:08.650 --> 00:25:12.110
If you do want to, if you're, if you have the chance to direct something and you want

527
00:25:12.110 --> 00:25:17.610
people to think you're good, do something visually unusual, which will flatter the intelligence.

528
00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:19.310
Do one odd thing.

529
00:25:19.490 --> 00:25:23.890
And every critic and every person will say, Ooh, you know, you're good.

530
00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:28.110
Whereas if you just shoot intuitively, like Harold Ramis, you know, is a great director.

531
00:25:28.110 --> 00:25:33.710
He directed a lot for us, but you know, his movies are classics, but no one talks about

532
00:25:33.710 --> 00:25:36.130
him as a great director because he didn't call attention to himself.

533
00:25:36.450 --> 00:25:40.090
Rob Reiner to me is the greatest director of our lifetimes.

534
00:25:40.230 --> 00:25:46.150
Because if you look at the movies he directed, they are the classics of their genres.

535
00:25:46.430 --> 00:25:50.050
He did Spinal Tap, which is the classic mockumentary.

536
00:25:50.490 --> 00:25:52.390
Stand By Me, classic coming of age.

537
00:25:52.510 --> 00:25:54.270
Princess Bride, classic family movie.

538
00:25:54.590 --> 00:25:55.910
Few Good Men, classic.

539
00:25:55.910 --> 00:25:58.910
And I don't just mean classic, I mean the epitome of their genre.

540
00:25:59.210 --> 00:26:01.550
Harry Met Sally, epitome of romantic comedy.

541
00:26:01.750 --> 00:26:07.070
And people don't talk about him, you know, because he just did it so right that he was

542
00:26:07.070 --> 00:26:07.430
invisible.

543
00:26:07.510 --> 00:26:13.870
If he had had like, you know, like an overhead camera in the ceiling fan and Harry Met Sally,

544
00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:17.590
everyone say, and he's such a great director, but you know.

545
00:26:18.290 --> 00:26:22.450
BJ, that reminds me of a piece of acting advice that I got that I use all the time.

546
00:26:22.610 --> 00:26:22.950
What?

547
00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:31.230
Um, so in any project, if it's a TV show or a movie, the, my acting coach said, you pick

548
00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:39.310
one moment for your character that you decide in this project is your big moment of realization,

549
00:26:39.930 --> 00:26:41.870
a confession, something.

550
00:26:42.030 --> 00:26:42.670
You pick one.

551
00:26:42.670 --> 00:26:51.270
And before you say that line, you pause, move forward, and then say it.

552
00:26:51.790 --> 00:26:53.810
And it'll blow people away.

553
00:26:53.890 --> 00:26:54.310
Your sunglasses.

554
00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:54.830
Yeah.

555
00:26:55.050 --> 00:27:01.270
And he said, even in auditioning, if you have just an audition scene, you pick one line in

556
00:27:01.270 --> 00:27:05.970
that audition scene where you pause and then you lean in before you say that line and you'll

557
00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:07.230
get the part every time.

558
00:27:07.590 --> 00:27:13.530
I swear to God, after I got that piece of acting advice, I went on a bunch of auditions.

559
00:27:13.730 --> 00:27:18.610
I got callbacks for like 12 auditions in a row when I did that little move in thing.

560
00:27:18.610 --> 00:27:19.790
And I still use it.

561
00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.170
I still use that.

562
00:27:21.170 --> 00:27:21.690
That's amazing.

563
00:27:22.230 --> 00:27:22.450
Okay.

564
00:27:22.590 --> 00:27:23.490
This is so funny to me.

565
00:27:23.570 --> 00:27:24.470
Is that in your book?

566
00:27:25.050 --> 00:27:31.230
Um, it's not in my book because I didn't want to steal his thunder.

567
00:27:31.490 --> 00:27:32.010
Oh, got it.

568
00:27:32.170 --> 00:27:34.190
That's Robert Devonzo, my acting coach.

569
00:27:34.330 --> 00:27:37.210
And I put him in the book and I said, call this guy and take his class.

570
00:27:37.210 --> 00:27:38.350
He'll give you the best advice.

571
00:27:38.550 --> 00:27:40.070
But I guess now I've put it on my podcaSt.

572
00:27:40.130 --> 00:27:41.210
So sorry, Robert.

573
00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:43.470
My acting coach did something similar.

574
00:27:43.690 --> 00:27:47.010
She was this very amazing older woman.

575
00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:49.810
And she said that she was auditioning for a movie.

576
00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:55.610
She was from France and she had an old, um, crown, like some dental work that as she was

577
00:27:55.610 --> 00:27:59.410
giving her big speech, she felt pop off in her mouth.

578
00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:06.190
And so she paused with her tongue to put it back in to cover her tooth back.

579
00:28:06.450 --> 00:28:10.070
And the pause was so long, the room became quiet.

580
00:28:10.450 --> 00:28:15.270
And then once she got her crown back over her tooth, she finished her line.

581
00:28:15.270 --> 00:28:20.050
And the casting director said that was the most powerful audition we have ever seen.

582
00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:21.350
She got the part.

583
00:28:22.290 --> 00:28:25.710
And so she was like, so sometimes, you know, a pause.

584
00:28:27.150 --> 00:28:29.110
That's my French impression.

585
00:28:29.330 --> 00:28:33.350
But, and it does, it's like, it's the one moment that doesn't make sense.

586
00:28:33.510 --> 00:28:35.030
And people are like, why doesn't that make sense?

587
00:28:35.550 --> 00:28:40.230
That's such a good way to distill the common thread of those two things.

588
00:28:40.470 --> 00:28:40.570
Yeah.

589
00:28:40.850 --> 00:28:41.130
Yeah.

590
00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:42.090
Well, okay.

591
00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:48.690
We actually wanted to talk to you also about what it was like to shoot those two days in

592
00:28:48.690 --> 00:28:49.150
the school.

593
00:28:49.310 --> 00:28:52.170
You had a huge, big group of kids in a classroom.

594
00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.290
What was it like directing such a big group like that?

595
00:28:57.190 --> 00:29:02.370
I guess an advantage of mine is I love talking to kids and groups of kids.

596
00:29:02.550 --> 00:29:07.410
Now, they were probably looking back 18 because the way the guilds work,

597
00:29:07.990 --> 00:29:14.190
you can work very different hours because of child labor for a kid over 18 or even over 16.

598
00:29:14.210 --> 00:29:15.570
The rules are very different.

599
00:29:15.770 --> 00:29:22.010
So you'll generally cast people who just turned 18 and look young or just turned 16 and look

600
00:29:22.010 --> 00:29:23.610
young for roles that are supposed to be younger.

601
00:29:23.790 --> 00:29:26.190
So those kids were probably slightly over 18.

602
00:29:26.590 --> 00:29:29.910
That said, they were still like, you know, they kid energy playing kid.

603
00:29:29.990 --> 00:29:31.530
I just love talking to kids.

604
00:29:31.850 --> 00:29:34.170
You know, when I read my kid's book, the book with no picture.

605
00:29:34.170 --> 00:29:36.430
So like a classroom, it's like the happiest I am.

606
00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:40.830
So I guess to me being like, all right, everybody, here's what happened.

607
00:29:41.110 --> 00:29:45.190
You know, this guy, Michael Scott, Steve over here, he did this.

608
00:29:45.310 --> 00:29:46.670
You know, they, they knew it.

609
00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:47.410
They got into it.

610
00:29:47.510 --> 00:29:51.230
Again, you talk to everyone, like their actors, including the background actors.

611
00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:54.610
Cause it was mostly background actors in the class.

612
00:29:54.790 --> 00:29:55.570
Most kids didn't speak.

613
00:29:55.570 --> 00:30:00.810
So just getting them, you know, in the mindset of it was really fun for me.

614
00:30:00.970 --> 00:30:02.710
They were real, you know, they had good spirit.

615
00:30:02.890 --> 00:30:03.410
They're professionals.

616
00:30:03.990 --> 00:30:09.470
And then I think it was Lee and Jean, because the writers are so involved on The Office or

617
00:30:09.470 --> 00:30:10.090
were so involved.

618
00:30:10.250 --> 00:30:15.450
I think it was, it was they who taught the kids like the chant and the dance and they

619
00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:16.010
were amazing.

620
00:30:16.290 --> 00:30:19.270
I love Steve's speech so much.

621
00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:21.650
I love his delivery of his speech.

622
00:30:25.570 --> 00:30:30.210
To tell them he's going to have to give this speech, but everything about it is so great.

623
00:30:30.430 --> 00:30:32.530
He tries to get them to figure it out.

624
00:30:32.650 --> 00:30:34.030
So he doesn't have to say it.

625
00:30:34.090 --> 00:30:34.390
Yeah.

626
00:30:35.290 --> 00:30:36.270
Which is amazing.

627
00:30:36.530 --> 00:30:38.030
And then he hands out the batteries.

628
00:30:38.550 --> 00:30:39.030
Lithium.

629
00:30:39.950 --> 00:30:40.430
Yeah.

630
00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:45.310
Not laptops or tuition, but some batteries for their laptops.

631
00:30:45.670 --> 00:30:45.950
Lithium batteries.

632
00:30:46.290 --> 00:30:46.390
Yeah.

633
00:30:46.650 --> 00:30:46.830
Yeah.

634
00:30:47.090 --> 00:30:50.330
Was that just Steve knowing Michael Scott so well?

635
00:30:50.610 --> 00:30:51.070
Yeah.

636
00:30:51.290 --> 00:30:51.470
Yeah.

637
00:30:51.610 --> 00:30:52.930
That was just Steve doing them so well.

638
00:30:52.930 --> 00:30:59.230
I, maybe someone pitched maybe the exact phrasing of, and what's better than laptops, but laptop

639
00:30:59.230 --> 00:31:00.670
battery, or what do you need for laptops?

640
00:31:00.850 --> 00:31:04.670
I don't know, but it's a collaboration because you see where Steve's going.

641
00:31:04.850 --> 00:31:08.690
So you might pitch him a line, but that was, yeah, that was Steve completely.

642
00:31:09.010 --> 00:31:12.890
You know, what also was great acting was in the photo where he's surrounded by the kids

643
00:31:12.890 --> 00:31:14.050
phrased on his desk.

644
00:31:14.170 --> 00:31:16.910
Like even that is good photo acting, his optimistic smile.

645
00:31:17.530 --> 00:31:17.970
Yes.

646
00:31:18.510 --> 00:31:19.530
Oh my gosh.

647
00:31:19.530 --> 00:31:23.470
He directed, and he said it was so much easier than acting.

648
00:31:24.190 --> 00:31:24.630
Really?

649
00:31:24.930 --> 00:31:28.570
And I thought, wow, that shows how much he's putting into the acting.

650
00:31:28.970 --> 00:31:35.450
I mean, this is, like always, he was the total leader of this episode.

651
00:31:35.690 --> 00:31:40.450
He had so much, I mean, he just had to thread that needle, and he did it so well.

652
00:31:40.810 --> 00:31:44.830
We do want to talk to you about the movie you have coming out that you directed.

653
00:31:45.170 --> 00:31:46.450
Can you share with us about it?

654
00:31:46.450 --> 00:31:47.190
It's cool.

655
00:31:47.350 --> 00:31:49.850
It's called Vengeance, and I wanted to do it.

656
00:31:49.950 --> 00:31:55.030
I saw a poster for a movie called Vengeance at the Cannes Film Festival, and I thought,

657
00:31:55.450 --> 00:31:57.610
I want to be in a movie called Vengeance.

658
00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:02.910
And I thought, but in a fun way, I was like, if I were in a movie, if you saw me on a movie

659
00:32:02.910 --> 00:32:05.250
poster in a movie called Vengeance, you'd know it was different.

660
00:32:05.630 --> 00:32:07.910
You know, it wouldn't be like, whoa, he's a cool action star.

661
00:32:07.990 --> 00:32:09.470
You'd be like, that's interesting.

662
00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:10.990
What would that, what's that tone?

663
00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:14.110
And honestly, that's where it came from at firSt.

664
00:32:14.250 --> 00:32:18.370
And then I had this plot idea that I just thought really cool.

665
00:32:18.790 --> 00:32:22.830
And yeah, so I took it to Blumhouse because they had just done Get Out, which I thought

666
00:32:22.830 --> 00:32:28.630
was another movie that they had been really popular despite having such challenging, interesting

667
00:32:28.630 --> 00:32:29.150
ideas.

668
00:32:29.350 --> 00:32:31.490
Because I didn't just want to make a thinky thing.

669
00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:36.450
I wanted it to be fun and funny and, you know, scary and all those things.

670
00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:40.770
And so, I mean, it was great to get to direct my first feature that had all of those elements,

671
00:32:40.990 --> 00:32:41.250
you know.

672
00:32:41.550 --> 00:32:44.850
Wait, so did you write it and direct it and you're in it?

673
00:32:45.490 --> 00:32:45.970
Mm-hmm.

674
00:32:46.330 --> 00:32:46.770
Wow.

675
00:32:46.830 --> 00:32:48.130
Triple threat, BJ.

676
00:32:48.350 --> 00:32:49.090
Oh, come on.

677
00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:53.310
Three very unthreatening professions, I will say, as a caveat.

678
00:32:53.910 --> 00:32:55.850
No one's scared of someone who does those three things.

679
00:32:56.090 --> 00:32:58.050
BJ, when does your movie come out?

680
00:32:58.250 --> 00:32:59.330
July 29th.

681
00:33:00.330 --> 00:33:01.210
Vengeance, out.

682
00:33:01.710 --> 00:33:04.190
BJ, thank you so much for coming on Office, ladies.

683
00:33:04.510 --> 00:33:05.330
We love you.

684
00:33:05.430 --> 00:33:06.130
I love it.

685
00:33:06.130 --> 00:33:06.890
I love it.

686
00:33:06.970 --> 00:33:07.850
I love you both.

687
00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:09.870
I don't see you enough.

688
00:33:10.130 --> 00:33:11.470
I love talking to you.

689
00:33:11.610 --> 00:33:15.330
I love remembering it from this lens with the two of you.

690
00:33:15.530 --> 00:33:16.130
It's great.

691
00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:22.790
You've always been one of my favorite people from the time I met you working on this show.

692
00:33:23.270 --> 00:33:24.870
So, I love you.

693
00:33:24.970 --> 00:33:29.850
Well, that is what a wonderful thing to say and I feel exactly the same way in immediate

694
00:33:29.850 --> 00:33:30.190
bonds.

695
00:33:30.330 --> 00:33:33.010
And I noticed, I was going to say, I noticed Angela's silence here.

696
00:33:33.010 --> 00:33:41.010
And I would say, I feel very similarly about Angela, but if it's not requited, then what

697
00:33:41.010 --> 00:33:41.770
are you going to do?

698
00:33:42.410 --> 00:33:45.370
But no, I felt that about both of you from the pilot, really.

699
00:33:46.250 --> 00:33:46.650
Okay.

700
00:33:46.870 --> 00:33:47.450
See you soon.

701
00:33:47.550 --> 00:33:48.150
Family for life.

702
00:33:48.350 --> 00:33:49.550
Family for life, BJ.

703
00:33:49.730 --> 00:33:50.450
Love you so much.

704
00:33:50.510 --> 00:33:50.610
Bye.

705
00:33:59.910 --> 00:34:01.710
Well, that was so fun, Angela.

706
00:34:02.230 --> 00:34:03.590
I love talking to BJ.

707
00:34:03.750 --> 00:34:07.150
I love that we got into that cool discussion about directing.

708
00:34:07.410 --> 00:34:11.130
I felt like it's when we had Mike Schur on and we were talking all about the writing process.

709
00:34:11.530 --> 00:34:12.090
I know.

710
00:34:12.250 --> 00:34:13.730
I could listen to BJ talk all day.

711
00:34:13.910 --> 00:34:14.270
Truly.

712
00:34:14.430 --> 00:34:15.350
He's so smart.

713
00:34:16.010 --> 00:34:17.370
I love him so much.

714
00:34:17.470 --> 00:34:18.130
I love his brain.

715
00:34:18.290 --> 00:34:18.890
I told him that.

716
00:34:19.050 --> 00:34:20.290
I said, BJ, I love your brain.

717
00:34:20.410 --> 00:34:21.690
Is that a weird thing to say to someone?

718
00:34:21.690 --> 00:34:23.150
No, I love his brain as well.

719
00:34:23.350 --> 00:34:25.150
It's so good to be back in the studio.

720
00:34:25.330 --> 00:34:25.990
Hi, Cassie.

721
00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:26.750
We missed you.

722
00:34:27.210 --> 00:34:28.430
Hi, welcome back.

723
00:34:28.770 --> 00:34:29.350
Thank you.

724
00:34:29.470 --> 00:34:33.710
Yes, I guess what we haven't shared with everyone was that we actually took a two-week

725
00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:34.910
break from the podcaSt.

726
00:34:35.150 --> 00:34:41.810
We made sure to double record episodes so that you didn't miss us, but we were off recording

727
00:34:41.810 --> 00:34:45.010
the audiobook for the Office BFFs.

728
00:34:45.170 --> 00:34:46.150
Oh, my gosh.

729
00:34:46.350 --> 00:34:48.770
You guys, I mean, I listen to audiobooks.

730
00:34:48.810 --> 00:34:51.050
I have a newfound respect for how they come together.

731
00:34:51.690 --> 00:34:54.370
We sat in a room together and read all day.

732
00:34:54.550 --> 00:35:00.110
It was a teeny tiny little, like, padded room, truly, and we read our book.

733
00:35:00.290 --> 00:35:02.530
One day at lunch, I had hummus.

734
00:35:02.690 --> 00:35:03.270
Oh, yes.

735
00:35:03.270 --> 00:35:07.510
And after lunch, my stomach kept making all these noises because I had a spicy hummus.

736
00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:08.850
It was so embarrassing.

737
00:35:09.110 --> 00:35:13.850
We had to stop the audiobook, and our amazing director, Dennis, had to go get a pillow and

738
00:35:13.850 --> 00:35:16.010
cover my stomach with a pillow, and he said, don't worry, Angela.

739
00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:16.910
It happens all the time.

740
00:35:17.250 --> 00:35:19.810
Yeah, and then the next day, it happened to me.

741
00:35:19.810 --> 00:35:22.110
So the next day, we're recording our audiobook.

742
00:35:22.250 --> 00:35:24.190
We both have pillows on our stomachs.

743
00:35:24.230 --> 00:35:24.710
Oh, my gosh.

744
00:35:24.710 --> 00:35:25.590
