A song and dance routine.

1237
00:59:06.620 --> 00:59:10.600
We had a fan question from Harmjen V in the Netherlands.

1238
00:59:11.480 --> 00:59:14.640
The dance routine of the Tots is awesome.

1239
00:59:15.220 --> 00:59:17.660
Where did you find these actors' dancers?

1240
00:59:18.020 --> 00:59:21.980
And Lisa from New Jersey asked who choreographed the dance routine.

1241
00:59:22.400 --> 00:59:26.600
Well, Randy Cordray shared with us that the choreographer was Tony Gonzalez.

1242
00:59:26.600 --> 00:59:31.820
And he had done choreography for Bring It On Again and Bring It On All or Nothing.

1243
00:59:32.120 --> 00:59:35.560
You know, he's currently doing the choreography for The Masked Singer.

1244
00:59:36.300 --> 00:59:39.340
And he cast the eight specialty dancers.

1245
00:59:39.340 --> 00:59:42.820
The dancers were Danielle E.

1246
00:59:42.940 --> 00:59:47.680
Hawkins, Glenda Morales, David Easel, Katrina Norman, Chris Moss,

1247
00:59:47.960 --> 00:59:51.840
Oscar Orozco, Jonathan Rice, and Quentin Burdett.

1248
00:59:52.220 --> 00:59:55.340
We only had two days of rehearsals with the dancers.

1249
00:59:55.740 --> 00:59:59.320
They set up a rehearsal space over on our warehouse stage.

1250
00:59:59.740 --> 01:00:01.100
They were so good.

1251
01:00:01.100 --> 01:00:02.320
They were so good.

1252
01:00:02.580 --> 01:00:07.200
There's no way in a million years that I could learn anything like that in two days.

1253
01:00:07.620 --> 01:00:08.080
No.

1254
01:00:08.300 --> 01:00:09.220
It's just a gift.

1255
01:00:09.400 --> 01:00:10.120
You just have it.

1256
01:00:10.460 --> 01:00:13.300
You know, I danced growing up.

1257
01:00:13.520 --> 01:00:16.100
There was a time when I could do that.

1258
01:00:16.260 --> 01:00:17.640
That time is not now anymore.

1259
01:00:18.280 --> 01:00:21.860
I tried during the pandemic to take up my tap dancing again.

1260
01:00:22.080 --> 01:00:23.520
I got a little tap dance floor.

1261
01:00:23.720 --> 01:00:24.180
I remember this.

1262
01:00:24.620 --> 01:00:25.400
And I was tapping.

1263
01:00:25.940 --> 01:00:30.280
And I thought, I'm going to learn a tap routine to footloose.

1264
01:00:30.280 --> 01:00:30.960
Oh.

1265
01:00:32.180 --> 01:00:34.260
And like, you got to cut loose.

1266
01:00:34.260 --> 01:00:34.540
Yeah.

1267
01:00:34.680 --> 01:00:35.020
Okay.

1268
01:00:35.200 --> 01:00:38.300
And I found like an instructional video on YouTube.

1269
01:00:39.020 --> 01:00:41.500
And I was like, this is going to be great.

1270
01:00:41.800 --> 01:00:42.860
It's going to be so fun.

1271
01:00:43.040 --> 01:00:45.320
I mean, it was like 45 seconds long.

1272
01:00:46.320 --> 01:00:46.800
Weeks.

1273
01:00:47.200 --> 01:00:49.900
Weeks I spent on this very beginner tap routine.

1274
01:00:50.160 --> 01:00:51.500
I never posted it.

1275
01:00:51.620 --> 01:00:55.840
I have like a gazillion videos of myself in my garage on my tap floor.

1276
01:00:56.080 --> 01:00:56.940
Can I please see one?

1277
01:00:57.040 --> 01:00:58.340
Can I please put it in pod stories?

1278
01:00:59.460 --> 01:01:01.140
Not to laugh at you.

1279
01:01:01.420 --> 01:01:02.600
Not to laugh at you.

1280
01:01:02.760 --> 01:01:05.840
To laugh with me because I was not even laughing yet.

1281
01:01:06.060 --> 01:01:08.440
So someone's laughing.

1282
01:01:09.400 --> 01:01:09.920
Maybe.

1283
01:01:10.240 --> 01:01:10.660
Maybe.

1284
01:01:10.920 --> 01:01:11.320
Okay.

1285
01:01:11.640 --> 01:01:14.100
Well, I thought the dancers did a fantastic job.

1286
01:01:14.240 --> 01:01:14.780
They did.

1287
01:01:15.340 --> 01:01:20.440
We also had a fan question from Laura in Warren, Ohio, who said, who wrote the song?

1288
01:01:20.500 --> 01:01:21.600
Hey, Mr. Scott.

1289
01:01:22.400 --> 01:01:27.020
Well, Lee and Jean wrote the lyrics for the song, and Eve Nelson wrote the music.

1290
01:01:27.600 --> 01:01:33.920
Eve is an Emmy-winning composer who did a lot of specialty work for us, such as composing

1291
01:01:33.920 --> 01:01:37.540
the music for Subtle Sexuality, the webisode.

1292
01:01:37.540 --> 01:01:39.100
The one we listened to last week.

1293
01:01:39.100 --> 01:01:40.780
Yes, that Mindy sings.

1294
01:01:41.520 --> 01:01:44.260
She also did all the music for Threat Level Midnight.

1295
01:01:44.660 --> 01:01:48.040
I thought we should hear an audio clip of Hey, Mr. Scott.

1296
01:01:48.460 --> 01:01:48.680
Ooh.

1297
01:02:27.140 --> 01:02:28.800
So good, Eve Nelson.

1298
01:02:29.080 --> 01:02:30.320
Eve coming through again.

1299
01:02:30.320 --> 01:02:32.580
And I can't wait now to see Threat Level Midnight.

1300
01:02:32.720 --> 01:02:34.120
I know, and see all that music.

1301
01:02:34.580 --> 01:02:38.320
It's funny that you bring up the webisodes because just last week we were talking about

1302
01:02:38.320 --> 01:02:43.120
it, but I also mentioned the bobbleheads last week that Joya Balfour, who worked for

1303
01:02:43.120 --> 01:02:47.100
NBC.com, was going to bring all these bobbleheads to the set, right?

1304
01:02:47.220 --> 01:02:47.440
Yeah.

1305
01:02:47.560 --> 01:02:49.420
For me to feature in Adventures with Angela.

1306
01:02:50.060 --> 01:02:54.040
Lady on set that day, she said, Angela, would you like all of these bobbleheads?

1307
01:02:54.740 --> 01:02:57.720
Like you can have one of all the cast members since you're doing this for us.

1308
01:02:57.780 --> 01:02:58.880
And I said, oh my God, I would.

1309
01:02:58.880 --> 01:03:00.540
And she goes, okay, let me, let me see.

1310
01:03:00.640 --> 01:03:04.360
But I think I can get you a whole set of full cast member bobbleheads.

1311
01:03:04.560 --> 01:03:06.120
And I was like, oh, that'll be so fun.

1312
01:03:06.220 --> 01:03:08.960
Maybe I'll like line them like on a shelf in my house.

1313
01:03:09.080 --> 01:03:09.840
That is so cool.

1314
01:03:09.980 --> 01:03:10.580
Do you have them?

1315
01:03:10.760 --> 01:03:11.060
Okay.

1316
01:03:11.160 --> 01:03:11.960
So here's the thing.

1317
01:03:12.140 --> 01:03:14.740
Years ago, she was like, Angela, I got the bobbleheads for you.

1318
01:03:14.820 --> 01:03:15.360
When do you want them?

1319
01:03:15.420 --> 01:03:16.300
And I was moving.

1320
01:03:16.740 --> 01:03:18.460
And I was like, oh, I'm moving right now.

1321
01:03:18.660 --> 01:03:19.420
Can we figure out?

1322
01:03:19.700 --> 01:03:20.580
And Jenna, I don't know.

1323
01:03:20.700 --> 01:03:21.340
Life happened.

1324
01:03:21.860 --> 01:03:22.600
Years went by.

1325
01:03:23.020 --> 01:03:27.560
Joya reached out to me and said, Angela, I've had these bobbleheads for 15 years.

1326
01:03:27.560 --> 01:03:27.920
What?

1327
01:03:28.420 --> 01:03:30.100
Just recently, she reached out.

1328
01:03:30.360 --> 01:03:31.500
She's like, they're in boxes.

1329
01:03:32.020 --> 01:03:33.220
Can I bring them to you?

1330
01:03:33.300 --> 01:03:35.700
I said, oh my gosh, Joya, yes.

1331
01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:41.000
So we met for coffee and we caught up 15 years of life and marriage and kids.

1332
01:03:41.220 --> 01:03:44.640
And Jenna, she opened up her trunk and it was full of bobbleheads.

1333
01:03:44.880 --> 01:03:47.220
I took a picture of her standing by her trunk.

1334
01:03:47.400 --> 01:03:50.480
I now have all of these bobbleheads.

1335
01:03:50.960 --> 01:03:54.560
That is so crazy because, you know, when we were going through all of our bins of stuff

1336
01:03:54.560 --> 01:04:02.020
for the book, I found an Oscar bobblehead and a Jim bobblehead and a Pam bobblehead

1337
01:04:02.020 --> 01:04:04.340
and a Dwight bobblehead.

1338
01:04:04.480 --> 01:04:05.240
That's all I have.

1339
01:04:05.420 --> 01:04:09.360
I will tell you the bobblehead I didn't know I need, but I definitely needed was Phyllis.

1340
01:04:09.820 --> 01:04:10.960
Oh, is Phyllis' good?

1341
01:04:11.120 --> 01:04:12.200
Phyllis' is so great.

1342
01:04:12.840 --> 01:04:17.100
Joya went on to tell me while we were having coffee, she said, you know, it's kind of crazy.

1343
01:04:17.100 --> 01:04:21.240
I've had all these bobbleheads, but I also have something that I think a lot of people

1344
01:04:21.240 --> 01:04:22.820
wanted, but I ended up getting.

1345
01:04:23.060 --> 01:04:24.320
And I was like, do tell.

1346
01:04:25.000 --> 01:04:27.740
Guess who has the original unicorn Barbie?

1347
01:04:28.680 --> 01:04:29.200
What?

1348
01:04:29.520 --> 01:04:29.980
Yes.

1349
01:04:30.120 --> 01:04:31.760
And here's Joya sharing about it.

1350
01:04:32.340 --> 01:04:36.700
The story of how I acquired the princess unicorn Barbie doll is really fun.

1351
01:04:37.020 --> 01:04:42.080
I, of course, was a digital producer for the show working at NBC.

1352
01:04:42.860 --> 01:04:48.880
And I had read the script for Moroccan Christmas, the episode that it appeared in, and I wanted

1353
01:04:48.880 --> 01:04:51.760
to use it for our Dunder Mifflin Infinity virtual game.

1354
01:04:51.860 --> 01:04:58.440
And I also wanted to build a website for Princess Unicorn because it seemed like this crazy, funny

1355
01:04:58.440 --> 01:05:02.080
thing that a little website would just be hilarious for.

1356
01:05:02.320 --> 01:05:06.580
If you're a big fan of the show, you know that we did a ton of these little one-off websites

1357
01:05:06.580 --> 01:05:07.520
all the time.

1358
01:05:08.260 --> 01:05:13.000
So princessunicorndoll.com was one of them.

1359
01:05:13.140 --> 01:05:18.840
And what's really interesting is that NBC let the domain expire some years ago and someone

1360
01:05:18.840 --> 01:05:22.680
bought it up and kept the exact same website there.

1361
01:05:22.680 --> 01:05:26.560
So you can actually go see the website and thank you to whoever bought that and kept the

1362
01:05:26.560 --> 01:05:27.340
same website up.

1363
01:05:27.440 --> 01:05:28.300
I appreciate you.

1364
01:05:28.680 --> 01:05:31.620
So I wanted to use it for the game.

1365
01:05:31.620 --> 01:05:35.920
It was a virtual item that you could purchase for your Dunder Mifflin office if you were

1366
01:05:35.920 --> 01:05:36.620
playing the game.

1367
01:05:36.740 --> 01:05:38.060
And it was a very rare item.

1368
01:05:38.240 --> 01:05:41.580
So the props department actually gave the doll to me.

1369
01:05:41.720 --> 01:05:46.640
They had two versions of the doll that they made, and they gave me one to photograph back

1370
01:05:46.640 --> 01:05:48.300
at my office at NBC.

1371
01:05:49.000 --> 01:05:50.280
So I did that.

1372
01:05:50.480 --> 01:05:54.420
And for whatever reason, no one at the show asked me for the doll back.

1373
01:05:54.980 --> 01:05:59.480
And the show ended, and everyone went on their separate ways.

1374
01:05:59.480 --> 01:06:04.760
And I eventually left NBC, and this doll came with me.

1375
01:06:04.860 --> 01:06:10.060
And it's been in my house as this kind of prized memento from the show.

1376
01:06:10.340 --> 01:06:12.920
You know, for almost 10 years, I've had this doll.

1377
01:06:13.120 --> 01:06:19.680
It's the one memento from the show I don't think I will ever give up because it's pretty

1378
01:06:19.680 --> 01:06:20.120
amazing.

1379
01:06:20.360 --> 01:06:23.140
So that is my Princess Unicorn Doll story.

1380
01:06:24.320 --> 01:06:29.060
I cannot believe she has one of the original.

1381
01:06:29.060 --> 01:06:31.840
They were only two, and she has one of them.

1382
01:06:32.560 --> 01:06:36.200
Well, now I know how to get original props.

1383
01:06:36.420 --> 01:06:37.260
You have to be like, you know what?

1384
01:06:37.260 --> 01:06:41.480
I'd love to build a website, but I'm going to need that to photograph it.

1385
01:06:41.500 --> 01:06:41.550
Yeah.

1386
01:06:41.660 --> 01:06:41.960
Mm-hmm.

1387
01:06:42.700 --> 01:06:47.620
And then I'm going to just wait for you to ask for it back.

1388
01:06:47.840 --> 01:06:49.820
And if you don't, you don't.

1389
01:06:50.040 --> 01:06:51.560
That is amazing.

1390
01:06:51.860 --> 01:06:52.220
Amazing.

1391
01:06:52.360 --> 01:06:53.580
It was so good to see Joya.

1392
01:06:53.700 --> 01:06:55.660
And Joya, thank you so much for the bobbleheads.

1393
01:06:55.660 --> 01:06:58.300
I'll have to like display them and show you guys.

1394
01:06:58.760 --> 01:07:01.980
Well, on that note, let's take a break.

1395
01:07:01.980 --> 01:07:04.780
And when we come back, we'll check in on Dwight.

1396
01:07:04.940 --> 01:07:05.920
We'll see what he's up to.

1397
01:07:06.160 --> 01:07:08.120
And then, oh boy.

1398
01:07:08.420 --> 01:07:09.940
Michael has a big announcement to make.

1399
01:07:10.060 --> 01:07:10.480
He does.

1400
01:07:21.610 --> 01:07:24.010
Dwight is now going around collecting money.

1401
01:07:24.290 --> 01:07:25.610
He starts with Kevin.

1402
01:07:25.790 --> 01:07:29.290
20 bucks to chip in for employee of the month per Jim's requeSt.

1403
01:07:30.090 --> 01:07:31.470
Oscar contributes as well.

1404
01:07:31.570 --> 01:07:32.790
He doesn't need Dwight's speech.

1405
01:07:32.990 --> 01:07:35.910
During this whole moment, guess who's playing solitaire?

1406
01:07:36.090 --> 01:07:36.810
I saw it.

1407
01:07:36.990 --> 01:07:38.750
I saw it once again.

1408
01:07:39.070 --> 01:07:41.530
Actively moving cards on her resale game.

1409
01:07:41.690 --> 01:07:46.030
Kate Flannery in the background as Meredith, playing a very quick game of solitaire.

1410
01:07:46.710 --> 01:07:49.970
Listen, Dwight's plan is five minutes ahead of schedule.

1411
01:07:50.210 --> 01:07:51.190
Right on schedule.

1412
01:07:51.570 --> 01:07:51.930
Mm-hmm.

1413
01:07:51.930 --> 01:07:52.810
It's working.

1414
01:07:53.630 --> 01:08:00.530
Back at the school, a teacher is up at the front of the room and she is like profusely

1415
01:08:00.530 --> 01:08:05.630
thanking Michael for following through on his promise to send these kids to school.

1416
01:08:06.110 --> 01:08:06.310
Yeah.

1417
01:08:06.650 --> 01:08:10.310
And then a student gets up and says, basically, you're my guardian angel.

1418
01:08:10.630 --> 01:08:10.950
Mm-hmm.

1419
01:08:11.650 --> 01:08:12.930
Michael is crying.

1420
01:08:13.550 --> 01:08:14.470
Oh boy.

1421
01:08:14.950 --> 01:08:16.870
It's just Michael.

1422
01:08:16.870 --> 01:08:26.130
The way they wrote the scene to build is so great because with each thing, you're getting

1423
01:08:26.130 --> 01:08:29.250
just a kick to the stomach and then it ends with Michael.

1424
01:08:29.770 --> 01:08:34.330
The woman who's giving this thank you speech was played by Monet McHale.

1425
01:08:34.430 --> 01:08:35.610
I hope I'm saying your name right.

1426
01:08:36.210 --> 01:08:37.130
She's amazing.

1427
01:08:37.470 --> 01:08:37.750
Mm-hmm.

1428
01:08:37.970 --> 01:08:40.370
She began her career as a musical theater actress.

1429
01:08:40.370 --> 01:08:46.770
She has been nominated for an NAACP Theater Award and she performed in an award-winning

1430
01:08:46.770 --> 01:08:47.790
one-woman show.

1431
01:08:48.010 --> 01:08:52.350
She's also had recurring roles on The Good Place and The Defenders as well as a ton of

1432
01:08:52.350 --> 01:08:53.890
other film and television roles.

1433
01:08:54.130 --> 01:09:00.770
Well, she set the bar for this scene because it's so heartfelt, her appreciation, as you

1434
01:09:00.770 --> 01:09:03.450
would be because you're sending these kids to college.

1435
01:09:03.650 --> 01:09:08.330
Like, the level of accountability that the scene starts with is like, oh, crap.

1436
01:09:08.610 --> 01:09:09.170
Michael.

1437
01:09:09.170 --> 01:09:10.050
Oh, Michael.

1438
01:09:10.590 --> 01:09:12.390
Well, it's Michael's turn to speak.

1439
01:09:13.210 --> 01:09:14.270
This is the moment.

1440
01:09:14.650 --> 01:09:14.890
Yeah.

1441
01:09:15.450 --> 01:09:16.550
The crowd is cheering.

1442
01:09:17.150 --> 01:09:19.210
Michael says he'll never forget today.

1443
01:09:19.850 --> 01:09:23.930
Oh, and then he asks the kids for a show of hands.

1444
01:09:24.210 --> 01:09:28.290
He wants to know, how many of you have ever made a stupid mistake?

1445
01:09:28.510 --> 01:09:34.130
He's trying to get them on his side before giving them this terrible news.

1446
01:09:34.430 --> 01:09:36.550
I kind of thought we needed to hear it.

1447
01:09:36.710 --> 01:09:37.290
Oh, no.

1448
01:09:37.490 --> 01:09:37.710
Really?

1449
01:09:38.510 --> 01:09:39.350
I don't know.

1450
01:09:39.550 --> 01:09:39.600
Oh, God.

1451
01:09:39.730 --> 01:09:40.230
Oh, God.

1452
01:09:40.550 --> 01:09:41.230
All right.

1453
01:09:41.950 --> 01:09:43.490
In this moment, I'm a Pam.

1454
01:09:43.710 --> 01:09:44.630
Okay, let's hear it.

1455
01:09:44.630 --> 01:09:51.950
I came here today because I promised you tuition, and tuition is very valuable, but you know

1456
01:09:51.950 --> 01:09:54.170
what's invaluable is intuition.

1457
01:09:54.590 --> 01:09:55.610
You know what that is?

1458
01:09:55.950 --> 01:09:59.870
That is the ability to know when something is about to happen.

1459
01:10:01.190 --> 01:10:03.190
Does anybody out there have intuition?

1460
01:10:03.530 --> 01:10:05.230
Know what's going to happen next?

1461
01:10:05.830 --> 01:10:06.250
Nobody?

1462
01:10:06.250 --> 01:10:06.690
Okay.

1463
01:10:07.190 --> 01:10:08.110
You're going to make me say it.

1464
01:10:08.510 --> 01:10:09.510
All right.

1465
01:10:09.810 --> 01:10:13.130
I am so proud of all of you.

1466
01:10:14.570 --> 01:10:21.490
Derek and LaFerve and Ben and Ayana and Michaela and Nikki and Jason.

1467
01:10:22.530 --> 01:10:23.670
And I'm sorry.

1468
01:10:23.790 --> 01:10:23.970
Okay.

1469
01:10:24.470 --> 01:10:24.990
Sorry, spacing.

1470
01:10:25.070 --> 01:10:25.530
Your name?

1471
01:10:25.850 --> 01:10:26.590
I'm Zion.

1472
01:10:26.790 --> 01:10:27.930
I'm Michaela's younger brother.

1473
01:10:28.170 --> 01:10:30.690
Well, Zion, I am not going to be paying for your tuition.

1474
01:10:34.370 --> 01:10:38.850
Which brings me to my main point, and that is that I will not be able to pay for anybody's

1475
01:10:38.850 --> 01:10:39.150
tuition.

1476
01:10:42.050 --> 01:10:43.250
I'm so, so sorry.

1477
01:10:46.130 --> 01:10:50.010
Listen, I'm sorry for all the people out there who skip Scott's Tots.

1478
01:10:50.170 --> 01:10:52.550
I just played the worst part for you.

1479
01:10:52.750 --> 01:10:54.310
You know, the cringiest part.

1480
01:10:54.550 --> 01:10:56.150
I watched this episode three times.

1481
01:10:56.330 --> 01:11:01.610
That's usually how many times I watch an episode as we prep each week for the podcast, and I might

1482
